    bodyHtml: `
<p>A sinus lift adds bone to the upper jaw near the sinus so that implants can be placed in the back of the mouth. It is a well-established procedure for patients who lack height in that area.</p><h2>When a sinus lift helps</h2><p>The upper back jaw often has limited bone height because the sinus sits above it, especially after tooth loss. A sinus lift gently raises the sinus membrane and adds bone to create room for an implant.</p><h2>Planning and recovery</h2><p>Imaging maps the sinus and bone before treatment. Healing allows the new bone to mature before the implant is placed. Your clinician will discuss what to expect and how to care for the area afterward.</p><h2>Working with an implant specialist in Vaughan</h2><p>Dr. Mehdi Adibrad is an experienced implant dentist and periodontist serving Vaughan and the surrounding area. If you would like a professional opinion on your options, the team at <a href="https://www.dfdentistry.ca/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Dr. Mehdi Adibrad at Dentin Family Dentistry</a> offers consultations for patients considering dental implants. The right plan depends on your bone, your gums and your overall health — a professional evaluation is the place to start.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is a sinus lift always needed for upper implants?</h3><p>No; it is only required when there is not enough bone height near the sinus.</p><h3>Is it a major procedure?</h3><p>It is routine for experienced clinicians; your provider will explain the specifics for your case.</p><h3>How long before I can have the implant?</h3><p>Often a few months of healing follow a sinus lift before implant placement.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional. Individual results vary, and treatment is determined following an examination.</em></p>
`,
