    bodyHtml: `
<p>Cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) gives a detailed 3D view of the jaw, teeth and surrounding structures. It is a cornerstone of accurate, safe dental-implant planning.</p><h2>What CBCT reveals</h2><p>Unlike a flat X-ray, a CBCT scan shows bone height, width and density, as well as nerves and the sinus. This information helps the clinician choose the right implant size and position.</p><h2>Why it improves planning</h2><p>With a 3D map, the team can plan around important structures and design a guide for precise placement. This reduces guesswork and supports a restoration that fits and functions well.</p><h2>Working with an implant specialist in Vaughan</h2><p>Patients in Vaughan are often referred to Dr. Mehdi Adibrad, a periodontist with advanced training in implantology. For readers in the Vaughan area, the team at <a href="https://www.dfdentistry.ca/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">the implant team at Dentin Family Dentistry</a> offers consultations for patients considering dental implants. A thorough consultation and examination are the clearest way to decide whether implants suit your situation.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is a CBCT scan necessary for implants?</h3><p>It is commonly used because the 3D detail greatly improves implant planning and safety.</p><h3>Is the scan quick?</h3><p>Yes, a CBCT scan is fast and non-invasive.</p><h3>Does it help avoid nerves?</h3><p>The 3D view helps the clinician plan around nerves and other structures.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional. Individual results vary, and treatment is determined following an examination.</em></p>
`,
