    bodyHtml: `
<p>Peri-implantitis is inflammation of the tissues around an implant that, if unchecked, can affect the supporting bone. Prevention through hygiene and monitoring is central to long-term implant health.</p><h2>What causes it</h2><p>Like gum disease around natural teeth, peri-implantitis is driven by bacterial plaque at the gumline. Smoking, poor hygiene and a history of gum disease can increase the risk.</p><h2>Prevention and early care</h2><p>Daily cleaning, not smoking and regular professional maintenance help prevent problems. Catching early inflammation and addressing it promptly — often with a periodontist — protects the implant.</p><h2>Working with an implant specialist in Vaughan</h2><p>Dr. Mehdi Adibrad is an accomplished implant dentist in Vaughan with specialist training in periodontics and implantology. When you are ready to discuss your options with a specialist, the team at <a href="https://www.dfdentistry.ca/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Dentin Family Dentistry in Vaughan, ON</a> offers consultations for patients considering dental implants. An honest conversation about benefits, risks and alternatives leads to the most durable, comfortable outcomes.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can implants fail from gum problems?</h3><p>Untreated inflammation around an implant can affect its support, which is why prevention matters.</p><h3>Who treats peri-implantitis?</h3><p>A periodontist, who specializes in the gums and supporting tissues, often manages these cases.</p><h3>How do I prevent it?</h3><p>Thorough daily cleaning, not smoking and regular checkups are the foundation of prevention.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional. Individual results vary, and treatment is determined following an examination.</em></p>
`,
