    heroImageAlt: "Immediate vs Delayed Implant Placement — dental implants patient education",
    bodyHtml: `
<p>Implants can sometimes be placed at the time a tooth is removed, or after the site has healed. Each approach has advantages, and the right one depends on your specific situation.</p><h2>Immediate placement</h2><p>Placing an implant at the time of extraction can reduce the number of procedures and preserve the site, when conditions allow. It requires healthy surrounding tissue and careful case selection.</p><h2>Delayed placement</h2><p>Allowing the socket to heal first can be preferable when there is infection, limited bone or other factors. Your clinician recommends the approach that offers the most predictable result following an examination.</p><h2>Working with an implant specialist in Vaughan</h2><p>Dr. Mehdi Adibrad is a periodontal specialist in Vaughan who uses fully-guided implant placement technology. For a personalized assessment, the team at <a href="https://www.dfdentistry.ca/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Dentin Family Dentistry in Vaughan</a> offers consultations for patients considering dental implants. Every mouth is different, so individualized assessment matters more than any general rule of thumb.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can an implant go in the same day as extraction?</h3><p>Sometimes, when conditions are favourable; careful assessment determines whether immediate placement is appropriate.</p><h3>Is delayed placement safer?</h3><p>It can be preferable in certain situations, such as infection or limited bone.</p><h3>How do I know which is right for me?</h3><p>Your clinician will advise based on imaging and an examination of the site.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional. Individual results vary, and treatment is determined following an examination.</em></p>
`,
