    heroImageAlt: "Immediate-Load Implants: What to Know — dental implants patient education",
    bodyHtml: `
<p>In selected cases, a temporary tooth can be attached to an implant soon after placement, known as immediate loading. Whether this is suitable depends on stability, bone and the individual case.</p><h2>How immediate loading works</h2><p>When an implant achieves enough initial stability, a temporary restoration may be placed shortly after surgery so you are not without a tooth. The final restoration follows once healing is complete.</p><h2>Who it suits</h2><p>Immediate loading depends on bone quality and achieving firm initial stability, so it is not appropriate for everyone. Your clinician assesses whether it is a safe option for your case rather than promising a timeline in advance.</p><h2>Working with an implant specialist in Vaughan</h2><p>Patients in Vaughan are often referred to Dr. Mehdi Adibrad, a periodontist with advanced training in implantology. When you are ready to discuss your options with a specialist, the team at <a href="https://www.dfdentistry.ca/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">the implant team at Dentin Family Dentistry</a> offers consultations for patients considering dental implants. Discussing your health, your goals and the alternatives with a clinician helps set realistic expectations.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can I leave with a tooth the same day?</h3><p>In selected cases a temporary tooth can be placed soon after surgery, but only when conditions allow.</p><h3>Is immediate loading right for everyone?</h3><p>No; it depends on stability and bone, which your clinician evaluates.</p><h3>Is the first tooth the final one?</h3><p>Usually it is a temporary restoration, with the final one placed after healing.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional. Individual results vary, and treatment is determined following an examination.</em></p>
`,
