    heroImageAlt: "Implants for Back Teeth (Molars) — dental implants patient education",
    bodyHtml: `
<p>Molars do the heavy lifting of chewing, so replacing a missing back tooth restores important function. Implants are a strong, stable way to fill these gaps.</p><h2>Why back teeth matter</h2><p>Missing molars can make chewing difficult and shift the bite over time. An implant restores a stable chewing surface and helps keep neighbouring and opposing teeth in position.</p><h2>Planning for chewing forces</h2><p>Because molars bear strong forces, the implant size, position and restoration are planned for durability. Bone volume in the back jaw is assessed, and grafting or a sinus lift is considered if needed.</p><h2>Working with an implant specialist in Vaughan</h2><p>Dr. Mehdi Adibrad is an accomplished implant dentist in Vaughan with specialist training in periodontics and implantology. For readers in the Vaughan area, the team at <a href="https://www.dfdentistry.ca/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Dentin Family Dentistry in Vaughan, ON</a> offers consultations for patients considering dental implants. Every mouth is different, so individualized assessment matters more than any general rule of thumb.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Do I need to replace a missing molar?</h3><p>Replacing it restores chewing and helps prevent neighbouring teeth from shifting.</p><h3>Can the back jaw support an implant?</h3><p>Often yes, though a sinus lift or graft is sometimes needed in the upper back jaw.</p><h3>Are molar implants strong enough to chew?</h3><p>Yes, implants are planned to handle normal chewing forces.</p><p><em>This article is general educational information and is not a substitute for personalized advice from a licensed dental professional. Individual results vary, and treatment is determined following an examination.</em></p>
`,
